---
// The inner-page template: a short dark hero, then prose. A new page is a copy of
// this file with new words; the layout, header, footer and access form come with it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro'
import { GITHUB_URL } from '../site'
---
<Base
  title="How Canopy works"
  description="How Canopy runs AI agents under human oversight: agents, runners, front doors, and the loop that turns a correction into a better agent."
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How it works</p>
      <h1>Agents do the work. People stay in the loop.</h1>
      <p>
        Canopy is the control plane around AI agents: it decides what runs, when and where, keeps
        the record of what happened, and puts every decision that needs a person in front of one.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>An agent is a repository</h2>
    <p>
      Each agent’s expertise lives in its own git repository: a persona, a turn procedure, and
      skills, the rules, examples and judgment that make someone good at a job, written down.
      Canopy holds the running instance: who owns it, who may use it, its credentials, its board
      of work and its history. Two instances can share one definition; changing a skill changes
      the agent everywhere it runs.
    </p>

    <h2>Runners execute, Canopy decides</h2>
    <p>
      A runner is a laptop or a cloud machine that starts Claude Code sessions for an agent, the
      way a CI runner is the machine that runs a build. Each agent has an ordered list of runners;
      if the first is offline or out of capacity, the next one takes the work. People who use an
      agent install nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Front doors people already use</h2>
    <dl>
      <dt>Email</dt><dd>Each agent can have its own mailbox, and answers in the thread.</dd>
      <dt>Slack</dt><dd>Mention an agent, or use its slash command; it replies in the thread.</dd>
      <dt>Web chat</dt><dd>Multiplayer sessions where several people and the agent share one conversation, live.</dd>
      <dt>Your application</dt><dd>An embeddable widget puts an agent inside another product, acting on what the visitor is looking at.</dd>
      <dt>MCP</dt><dd>Every Canopy API is also an MCP tool, so an AI assistant can drive Canopy as the person signed in.</dd>
    </dl>

    <h2>Who is asking decides what the agent may do</h2>
    <p>
      Every turn records who asked and how sure Canopy is of it. An agent’s owner and admins reach
      all of it; anyone else reaches only what the agent publishes for them, and with nothing
      published, nothing at all. A person sets each agent to manual or auto. In manual mode, nothing
      goes out until a person has seen it.
    </p>

    <h2>A correction improves the agent</h2>
    <p>
      Sessions are recorded and shared, so people learn by watching each other work with an agent.
      AI reviews those sessions for what went wrong, and proposes a change to the agent’s skills or
      rules. A person approves it from the Supervisor inbox, and the fix reaches every agent it
      applies to. The mistake is fixed once, not corrected every time.
    </p>

    <h2>Open source</h2>
    <p>
      Canopy is developed in the open by Dimagi under the BSD 3-Clause License.
      <a href={GITHUB_URL}>Read the code on GitHub</a>, or
      <a href="#request-access" data-beta-open>request access</a>. Access is currently closed to Dimagi and its partners.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
